    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. My main worry is that using \"Unknown\" everywhere feels a bit reductive, especially when the maps themselves contain depictions of strange or unidentifiable figures, symbols, or even creatures that don't clearly fit into known ethnographic or biological categories. Beyond just the human 'creator' field, how should we approach the metadata for the *subjects* of the maps when they are anomalous or defy standard controlled vocabularies? Is there a way to tag something as 'unidentified' that acknowledges its unconventional or mysterious nature without violating professional standards?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely right to worry about the reductive nature of \"Unknown,\" especially when dealing with maps that depict entities that might be real, mythical, or simply the result of a cartographer's hallucination. In the world of Dublin Core, you can't just invent a new term, but you can use the `subject` element with carefully chosen qualifiers to capture that mystery without breaking the rules.\n\nInstead of a flat \"Unknown,\" you could use the value \"Unidentified entity\" or \"Unknown creature\" within the `subject` field, perhaps cross-referenced with a note in the `note` element. This acknowledges the anomaly while adhering to the standard. If the figures are clearly mythical, you might tag them as \"Mythological entity\" or \"Unidentified creature,\" which allows for future researchers to refine the classification as more context is found.\n\nIt is also worth considering the `description` element to add a brief, descriptive note about the nature of the figures, such as \"Figures of unknown origin, possibly depicting local folklore or speculative biology.\" This adds depth to the metadata without cluttering the controlled vocabulary fields.\n\nIf you are using a specific controlled vocabulary like LCSH (Library of Congress Subject Headings",
      "source": "target",
